            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Salt Lake City to Hawaii?</h3>
            <p>Late August through November and January offer the lowest fares, typically $350 to $650 round-trip. For winter escapes, book 60 to 90 days ahead and target January after the holidays. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing year-round.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Salt Lake City to Hawaii?</h3>
            <p>Delta Air Lines, Southwest Airlines, and Hawaiian Airlines all operate daily year-round nonstop flights from Salt Lake City to Honolulu. Delta and Southwest also offer seasonal nonstop service to Maui. Flight duration is approximately six to seven hours across the Pacific.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a passport to visit Hawaii from Salt Lake City?</h3>
            <p>No passport is required for United States citizens traveling to Hawaii. Hawaii is the 50th state, so standard domestic travel rules apply. A government-issued photo ID or driver's license suffices for TSA screening at Salt Lake City International Airport.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Salt Lake City to Hawaii flight?</h3>
            <p>Southwest allows two free checked bags. Most other carriers charge $35 for the first checked bag up to 50 pounds, plus a carry-on and personal item. Surfboards and golf clubs may incur additional fees on Delta and United but fly free on Southwest.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Honolulu Airport to Waikiki?</h3>
            <p>The Bus Route 20 costs $3 and reaches Waikiki in 45 to 60 minutes. SpeediShuttle shared rides cost $15 to $18. Taxis and Uber run $35 to $50 and take 20 to 35 minutes depending on H-1 freeway traffic conditions.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I island-hop after flying into Honolulu from Salt Lake City?</h3>
            <p>Yes, inter-island flights connect Honolulu to Maui, Kauaʻi, and the Big Island in 25 to 45 minutes. Hawaiian Airlines and Southwest operate frequent island-hopper service. No additional TSA screening is required for connecting flights within Hawaii.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Hawaii safe for tourists from Salt Lake City?</h3>
            <p>Hawaii is generally very safe, though petty theft occurs at beaches and tourist areas. Ocean safety requires more attention than crime; strong currents, rip tides, and sharp coral pose real risks. Always heed warning flags and lifeguard instructions at all beaches.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best Hawaiian island to visit for first-time travelers from Salt Lake City?</h3>
            <p>Oahu offers the most accessible introduction with Waikiki and Pearl Harbor. Maui balances resorts and natural beauty; the Big Island appeals to volcano enthusiasts. Kauaʻi suits adventure seekers. Choose based on whether you prefer city energy, beaches, or wilderness.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Salt Lake City International for my Hawaii flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive two hours before scheduled departure for domestic flights from SLC. During holiday weekends and winter peak season, consider arriving two and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Hawaii is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must rebook passengers on the next available flight at no extra cost for weather or operational delays. Unlike international routes, EU compensation rules do not apply. Maintain comprehensive travel insurance for accommodation and activity reimbursement protection.</p>
















